049803 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "15363df8-84f0-5d8e-be74-122c5c580d0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1031 "The Nth Term of an Arithmetic Sequence" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1033 668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050157 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "32ad96da-6870-504d-ad58-7d14df8bea1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105475 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12924" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105475 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence given that the first term is $-1$ and the eighth term is $13.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105475 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7b51055-36f2-552b-817e-3632cf0446b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105475 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is $a_1=-1,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_1=-1$ and $a_8=13$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{1}}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-(-1)}{8-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ta_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = -1 + (n-1) (2) \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = -1 + 2n - 2 \\[3pt]\n\t\t& = 2n - 3.\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105476 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105476 #edb/ref 1071 "The fifth and sixth terms of an arithmetic sequence are $46$ and $50,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7bb25e3-0f81-5290-970d-6785ecbc921c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105476 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nSince the terms $a_5=46$ and $a_6=50$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{6}-{a}_{5} \\\\  & =50-46 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_5=46,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_5$ is $4$ jumps after $a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na_5 &= a_1 + 4d \\[3pt]\n46 &= a_1 + 4\\cdot 4\\[3pt]\n46 &= a_1 +16 \\[3pt]\n46-16 &= a_1\\[3pt]\n      a_1&= 30.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\ta_n&= a_{1}+(n-1)d \\[3pt]\n \t\t& = 30 + (n-1) \\cdot 4 \\[3pt]\n \t\t& =30 + 4n -4 \\[3pt]\n\t\t& = 4n +26.\n\t}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105477 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12927" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105477 #edb/ref 1071 "An arithmetic sequence has the $3$rd term $a_{3}=9$ and the $8$th term is $a_{8}=69.$ Find a formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e564d562-6dd0-5736-a539-63b6c6592e81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105477 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out what the first term $a_1$ and the common difference $d$ are.\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms $a_{8}=69$ and $a_{3}=9$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{8}-{a}_{3}}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{69-9}{8-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{5} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, $a_{3}=9,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since $a_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after $a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}+2\\cdot 12 \\\\ 9 & ={a}_{1}+24 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-15+(n-1)\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-15+12n-12 \\\\  & =12n-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105919 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105919 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105919 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105919 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Nth Term Given the First Term and Another Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105919 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105919 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3b13a2e9-d7e8-51a3-b452-0594858dd0d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105920 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105920 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186527223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105920 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Nth Term Given Two Consecutive Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105920 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105476 #edb/ref 1759