 is ${a}_{1}=0,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$ \n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{1}=0$ and ${a}_{9}=40$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{9}-{a}_{1}}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-0}{9-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{8} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =0+(n-1)(5) \\\\  & =5n-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527130 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527130 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527130 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527130 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $7$th term is $-18$ and the first term is $6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527130 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bf79e97-49c2-5006-b045-7fdc65e739fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527130 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nWe already know that the first term is ${a}_{1}=6,$ so we just need to figure out the common difference $d.$ \n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{1}=6$ and ${a}_{7}=-18$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{7}-{a}_{1}}{7-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-18-6}{7-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-24}{6} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,  the formula for the $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =6+(n-1)(-4) \\\\  & =6-4n+4 \\\\  & =10-4n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527133 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527133 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527133 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527133 #edb/ref 1071 "The $7$th and $8$th terms of an arithmetic sequence are $-13$ and $-20,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527133 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1617bc78-d7dd-5411-87bd-2e3b8377e377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527133 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nSince the terms ${a}_{7}=-13$ and ${a}_{8}=-20$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{8}-{a}_{7} \\\\  & =-20-(-13) \\\\  & =-20+13 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{8}=-20,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{8}$ is $7$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & ={a}_{1}+7d \\\\ -20 & ={a}_{1}+7(-7) \\\\ -20 & ={a}_{1}-49 \\\\ -20+49 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =29+(n-1)(-7) \\\\  & =29-7n+7 \\\\  & =36-7n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527142 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527142 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527142 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527142 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $14$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $3$rd term is $40$ and the $23$rd term is $-20.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527142 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "280035db-18a9-57e4-8578-03884e2e2b26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527142 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\n \nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{3}=40$ and ${a}_{23}=-20$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{23}-{a}_{3}}{23-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20-40}{23-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-60)}{20} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{3}=40,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{3}$ is $2$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}+2d \\\\ 40 & ={a}_{1}+2(-3) \\\\ 40 & ={a}_{1}-6 \\\\ 40+6 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =46+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =46-3n+3 \\\\  & =49-3n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $14$th term by substituting $n=14$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{14} & =49-3(14) \\\\  & =49-42 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527197 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527197 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527197 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527197 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the  $11$th term of an arithmetic sequence if the $19$th term is $99$ and the $4$th term is $-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527197 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae437285-daa1-5ee1-861c-3f25e99e891f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527197 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$\n\nTo find the common difference, we can find the total difference between the terms ${a}_{19}=99$ and ${a}_{4}=-6$ and divide it by the number of jumps, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{19}-{a}_{4}}{19-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{99-(-6)}{19-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{105}{15} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{4}=-6,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{4}$ is $3$ jumps after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+3d \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+3\\cdot 7 \\\\ -6 & ={a}_{1}+21 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-27+(n-1)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7n-34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find the $11$th term by substituting $n=11$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & =7(11)-34 \\\\  & =77-34 \\\\  & =43\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527223 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67315" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527223 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527223 #edb/ref 1071 "The second and third terms of an arithmetic sequence are $45$ and $80,$ respectively. Determine the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527223 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e88dff2d-42e3-57bd-b14e-035af9fb2260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527223 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term is given by  \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d,\n$$\n\nso we need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}$ and the common difference $d.$ \n\nSince the terms ${a}_{2}=45$ and ${a}_{3}=80$ are consecutive, we can subtract them to find the common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{3}-{a}_{2} \\\\  & =80-45 \\\\  & =35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use one of our known values, say, ${a}_{2}=45,$ to determine the first term of the sequence. Since ${a}_{2}$ is $1$ jump after ${a}_{1},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={a}_{1}+1\\cdot d \\\\ 45 & ={a}_{1}+35 \\\\ 45-35 & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence is:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 35 \\\\  & =10+35n-35 \\\\  & =35n-25\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
